{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 109 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6baa7fd401f5ead69c8fb98275280ee6b2baac3d66f6cebac52a2a525dfd672c" :edb/db-before-t 108 :edb/db-after-t 109 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533421 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:00.279Z" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323901 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323902 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323903 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323898 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323899 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323900 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323904 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054821 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323914 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323777 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323778 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323907 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323908 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186054830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323869 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052695 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186055248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323384 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055248 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A = {{1}, {2, 3}, {1, 3, 6}}$?\n\n1. ${2, {1}}$\n2. ${{2, 3}}$\n3. ${{-1}, {1, 3, 6}}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186055248 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={{1},{2,3},{1,3,6}}?$\n\n- ${2,{1}}$\n- ${{2,3}}$\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055248 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each option.\n\n- ${2,{1}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element $2\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\n- ${{2,3}}$ is a subset of $A.\\,✓$\n\n- ${{-1},{1,3,6}}$ is *not* a subset of $A$ because it contains the element ${-1}\\notin A.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052707 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186055250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323437 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055250 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to $Q^{-}$?\n\n1. $1$\n2. $-\\sqrt{18}$\n3. $\\sqrt{6}$\n4. $0$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186055250 1071 "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n\n- $1$\n- $-\\sqrt{18}$\n- $\\sqrt{6}$\n- $0$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186055250 1074 "Remember that ${Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $1$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{18}=-3\\sqrt{2}$ is a not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\sqrt{6}$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $0$ is not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None of those listed\"." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186059678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323811 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186059678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323812 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186059763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323771 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323980 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323981 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186060282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058284 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186060282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323613 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186060282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186060282 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y+z=2 \\\\ x-6y+11z=3 \\\\ 3x-4y-z=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & a \\\\ 1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186060282 1071 "The system of equations\n${3x - 4y + z = 2; x - 6y + 11z = 3; 3x - 4y - z = 4$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & a \\\\ 1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the values of $a, b$ and $c$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186060282 1074 "We can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=-6,\\,c=11.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323711 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323714 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323774 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323733 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064250 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323734 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323962 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186064324 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323966 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323939 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323940 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323947 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323948 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323763 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323764 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323765 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323972 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323968 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323974 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323710 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323757 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323762 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323772 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323787 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323788 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323732 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323737 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323848 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323845 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066856 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323268 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069286 1071 "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{5b + 8}{12b^{2}} =-\\frac{12}{36b} + \\frac{4b + 8}{12b^{2}}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069286 1071 "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}=-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069286 1074 "We note that $b=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $36b$ and $12{b}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(36b,12{b}^{2})=36{b}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $36{b}^{2},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}} & =-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}} \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b}+\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =36{b}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+36{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) & =b\\cdot 36b\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{36b})+3\\cdot 12{b}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{12{b}^{2}}) \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{5b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) & =b\\cdot \\cancel{36b}\\cdot (-\\frac{12}{\\cancel{36b}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{12{b}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{4b+8}{\\cancel{12{b}^{2}}}) \\\\ 3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(5b+8) & =-12b+3(4b+8) \\\\ 15b+24 & =-12b+12b+24 \\\\ 15b+24 & =24 \\\\ 15b & =24-24 \\\\ 15b & =0 \\\\ b & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. Since $b=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066868 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323249 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069288 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069288 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{x^{2} - 4x + 7}dx$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069288 1074 "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+7 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+7 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+7 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4x+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-2)/\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3}})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066904 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323361 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069294 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int(15 - 25x)/((2x^{2} - 6x + 9)(x - 4))dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186069294 1071 "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186069294 1074 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD=(-6)^{2}-4(2)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{C}{x-4}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n15-25x=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=10,B=-15$ and $C=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}=\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10x-15}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}+\\frac{-5}{x-4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the substitution $u=2{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(4x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2{x}^{2}-6x+9| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15-25x}{(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{2x-3}{2{x}^{2}-6x+9}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9))-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-6x+9)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186078885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076058 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186078885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323334 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186078885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186078885 1071 "A medical doctor records the temperature of $100^{∘}F$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $64^{∘}F$, which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186078885 1071 "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186078885 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323782 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323783 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323784 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323785 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323786 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323858 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323859 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083249 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323804 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323719 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186087753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323960 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085548 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186088154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323346 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088154 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$. If $v_{1} + v_{2} = 0$ and $T(v_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}54 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}$, find $T(21v_{1} + 18v_{2})$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186088154 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(21{v}_{1}+18{v}_{2}).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088154 1074 "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21{v}_{1}+18{v}_{2} & =18{v}_{1}+3{v}_{1}+18{v}_{2} \\\\  & =18({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+3{v}_{1} \\\\  & =18(0)+3{v}_{1} \\\\  & =3{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(21{v}_{1}+18{v}_{2})=T(3{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3{v}_{1}) & =3T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}162 \\\\ -108 \\\\ 90\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(21{v}_{1}+18{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}162 \\\\ -108 \\\\ 90\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085566 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186088157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323641 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088157 1071 "If $T$ is a linear transformation and $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186088157 1071 "If $\\,T\\,$ is a linear transformation and $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186088157 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=3[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]) & =T(3[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=3T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323809 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323810 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323857 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323855 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323806 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090340 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186092695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323424 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092695 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186092695 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092695 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-8)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090352 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186092697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323247 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092697 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$Q = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186092697 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186092697 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 2 & -10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(-10-\\lambda )-(-3)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 60+6\\lambda +10\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+16\\lambda +66 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={16}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 66 \\\\  & =256-264 \\\\  & =-8 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions, and therefore, $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323789 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323766 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323767 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323768 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323779 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323780 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323781 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323793 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323794 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186096839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323795 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [175