n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4x+8y \\\\ -3x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\,T\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-4x+8y \\\\ -3x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123296 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186125121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323362 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125121 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{3}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186125121 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125121 1074 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{11}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-11{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3}=11\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}-5(-1+{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2-{x}_{3} \\\\ -1+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123308 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186125123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323577 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125123 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{4}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} - x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{2} - x_{1} + 3x_{3} + 4x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} - 3x_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [1759218612