 #edb/ref 17592186323256 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125125 1071 "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186125125 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186125125 1074 "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129085 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323799 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129122 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323977 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323958 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323969 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129126 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323976 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323854 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323872 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323913 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129133 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323918 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129134 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323921 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323873 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323919 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323920 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323890 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323967 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323938 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323970 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323971 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129185 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323856 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323941 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323982 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127069 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323182 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129603 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x, y, z) = e^{-x^{2} - y^{2} - z^{2}}$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129603 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129603 1074 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $0<c\\le 1$ since $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c$ must be non-negative. If we let $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡c={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127093 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323278 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129607 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129607 1071 "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 7$, then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129607 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127111 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323444 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129610 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x, y, z) = \\ln (x + 2y + 3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1, 1, 0)$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129610 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,0).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129610 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2(1)+3(0))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x+2y+3z & =3 \\\\ x+2y+3z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ is the plane $x+2y+3z-3=0.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127129 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323332 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129613 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129613 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-3$ and $λ_{2} = 2$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $v_{3} = [a, b, c]^{T}$ such that the set ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129613 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127147 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323427 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129616 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = y^{2}e^{2x} - 1$ at the point $(0, 2, 3)$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129616 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1$ at the point $(0,2,3).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129616 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{2x}-1) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,2) & =2(2)^{2}{e}^{2(0)}=8 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,2) & =2(2){e}^{2(0)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,2) \\\\ z-3 & =8(x-0)+4(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =8x+4y-8 \\\\ -8x-4y+z+5 & =0 \\\\ 8x+4y-z-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127153 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323457 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129617 1071 "Consider the surface $\\ln (2x^{3} + yz) = 3$. Given that $n = ⟨a, b, c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e, e^{2},-e)$, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129617 1071 "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz)=3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(e,{e}^{2},-e),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129617 1074 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(e,{e}^{2},-e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}+yz))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot 6{x}^{2})i+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot z)j+(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\cdot y)k \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{z}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{y}{2{x}^{3}+yz}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(e,{e}^{2},-e) & =\\frac{6{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(-e)}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }j+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}(-e)}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{e}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{e}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\frac{6}{e},\\,-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{e})}{(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}=-6e.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127243 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323365 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129632 1071 "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sin θ}2r\\cos θdrdθ$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129632 1071 "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129632 1074 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127249 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323195 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129633 1071 "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129633 1071 "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$∬_(D)2xydA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129633 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127261 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323626 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129635 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u\\cos v \\\\ 3u\\sin v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129635 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129635 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127279 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323336 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129638 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y \\ge 0$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186129638 1071 "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186129638 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323935 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186133841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323957 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186133842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323959 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323936 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186133846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323973 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131203 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323547 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134199 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dA,\\,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134199 1071 "Calculate $∬_(D)\\sin (x + y)dA$, where $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, 0 \\le y \\le x}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134199 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le x}.$ \n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{0}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131209 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323580 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134200 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}^{5}\\,dy\\,dx=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134200 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sin x}^{1 + \\sin x}x^{5}dydx$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134200 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}^{5}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{5}[{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{5}[y]_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{5}((1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{5}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{5}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\pi )^{6}}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\pi }^{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131221 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323231 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134202 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{π/2}}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{y^{2}}\\sin y^{3}dxdy$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186134202 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186134202 1074 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186147447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323829 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186147447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323832 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186147448 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323831 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186147449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323830 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186152590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150017 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186152590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323186 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186152590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186152590 1071 "What is the dimension of $R_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186152590 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186152590 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{7}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{7}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{7}[t])=7+1=8.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323773 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323814 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157436 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323815 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323816 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157438 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323817 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155511 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186157770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323407 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157770 1071 "If $A = {3, 4, 5, 6, 9, 10, 15}$ and $B = {3, 4, 5, 7}$, then $A∖B =$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186157770 1071 "If $A={3,4,5,6,9,10,15}$ and $B={3,4,5,7},$ then $A∖B=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157770 1074 "The set $A∖B$ is the set of all elements belonging to $A$ but *not* $B.$ Note that the elements $3,4,5\\in B$ are also in $A,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∖B & =A-B \\\\  & ={3,4,5,6,9,10,15}-{3,4,5,7} \\\\  & ={6,9,10,15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155529 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186157773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323447 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157773 1071 "Let $A$ be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $B$ be the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0$. What is $B∖A$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186157773 1071 "Let $A$ be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $B$ be the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0.$ What is $B∖A?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186157773 1074 "The multiples of $10$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA={10,20,30,40,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $B∖A$ is the *difference* of $B$ and $A,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $B$ but *not* in $A.$\n\nThe elements of $B$ that are also in $A$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $10$). So, we must remove these elements from $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∖A & =B-A \\\\  & ={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,…}-{10,20,30,40,…} \\\\  & ={5,15,25,35,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323892 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323891 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323834 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323835 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323836 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186166887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323837 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165069 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186167315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323585 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167315 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167315 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n$B_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, B_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf $B = {B_{1}, B_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{B_{1}, B_{2}}$, and $[A]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $x_{1} + x_{2} =$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186167315 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1} & 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165081 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186167317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323537 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167317 1071 "Let $f(x, y) = 2x + xy$, where $x = se^{s}$ and $y = te^{s}$. Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186167317 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=2x+xy,$ where $x=s{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186167317 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186175931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323684 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186175932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323686 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186175932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323687 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186175961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323689 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186179654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323922 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186179811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323703 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186179813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323705 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186179813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323706 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323712 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323681 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323674 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323675 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323676 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323677 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323678 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323679 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323802 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323803 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183181 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186184767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323654 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184767 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x ∈ R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n> *$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $_________$.*" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186184767 1071 "Let $W$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree $5$ or greater. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184767 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$W$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **$W$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, the zero polynomial has degree $0$, which is less than $5.$\n\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- For any polynomial $p(x)$ of degree $5$ (or greater), the corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ has the same degree. $\\,✓$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183211 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186184772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323440 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184772 1071 "If $E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$, then $E^{4} =$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186184772 1071 "If $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, then ${E}^{4}=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186184772 1074 "First, we compute ${E}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{2} & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${E}^{2}=E,$ so $E$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${E}^{4}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{4} & ={E}^{2}\\cdot {E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\cdot E \\\\  & ={E}^{2} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{E}^{4}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186192786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323709 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186192787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323708 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186192790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323718 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186192796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323717 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191847 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323532 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193325 1071 "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{2}$ or $R^{3}$?" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193325 1071 "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193325 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191853 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323298 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193326 1071 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y^{2} - \\ln (xz)$, evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1, 0, 1)$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193326 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193326 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-(0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2(1)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191859 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323505 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193327 1071 "For the function $f(x, y) = e^{xy} + x^{2}y$, evaluate $f_{y}(1, 0)$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186193327 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y,$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(1,0).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186193327 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+{x}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}\\cdot (1)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =x{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186198953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197558 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186198953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323541 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186198953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186198953 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} + v^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186198953 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186198953 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-2v\\cdot v \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}={u}^{2}$ defines a pair of intersecting lines $v=u$ and $v=-u$ in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323818 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323819 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323820 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323821 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323822 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323823 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323824 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323825 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186229156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323826 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186234011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323544 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186234016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323635 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186234016 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{3x}-1]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{e}^{3x}}{1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 false] [17592186234016 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligne