3 1079 #edb/ref 17592186322572 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323673 1080 #uuid "ffb275cd-5bbf-5cf9-9048-108f7625e28d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323190 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323325 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323329 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1069 "q-353900" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $(x+1){e}^{x}$ about $x=-2.$\n\n$(x+1){e}^{x}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+2)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+2)^{3}+⋯$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1072 #uuid "33775637-f531-50c1-8901-10dfd2293327" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323674 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=(x+1){e}^{x}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+1){e}^{-2}=-{e}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(x+1){e}^{x}=(2+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =(2-2){e}^{-2}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(2+x){e}^{x}=(3+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =(3-2){e}^{-2}={e}^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x}+(3+x){e}^{x}=(4+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =(4-2){e}^{-2}=2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){e}^{x} & =-{e}^{-2}+(0)(x+2)+\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2}}{3!}(x+2)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3{e}^{2}}(x+2)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323178 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323295 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323492 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1069 "q-353904" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1072 #uuid "17018abc-8b11-54e7-a5d9-de971a73a6ea" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323675 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323184 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323192 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323253 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323285 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1069 "q-353905" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1071 "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1072 #uuid "0ab2fb88-19f2-579a-b40e-6799452a719f" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323676 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323255 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323348 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323372 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1069 "q-353917" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}\\,+⋯$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1072 #uuid "1ea0e4bd-2ba0-5ea8-a5c9-cf5026dd4498" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323677 1074 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323552 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1069 "q-354017" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1071 "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=2\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1072 #uuid "4898f317-a93b-5fce-a3d4-da1e62f1015b" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323678 1074 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=2\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =(1)+(0)(x-2\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-2\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-2\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(1)}{4!}(x-2\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{4}}{24}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5) & ≈1-\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{4}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.29,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323664 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1069 "q-354043" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1071 "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1072 #uuid "9ea0e9ed-64e4-52f1-b7df-82d492346b3d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323679 1074 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-{csc}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-1+(-2)(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-2(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2$ into the above to approximate $cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2) & ≈-1-2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+0.2-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-1-2(0.2) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323432 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1069 "q-35425" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$  is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1072 #uuid "9288acd6-ddf2-55d8-ba92-e1c27cfdfe7c" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323680 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323633 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1069 "q-35434" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}.\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1072 #uuid "98e375b6-3e6d-5b62-8ce3-1cf16ed1d92d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323681 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)={{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323531 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1069 "q-35452" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0 & 20 & -35 & 24\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1072 #uuid "b0d60236-44f8-5ff8-95cf-eb01e33fc186" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323682 1074 "First, we need to convert $D$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 0 & 20 & -35 & 24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 4 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $D$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323198 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1069 "q-35453" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1072 #uuid "9f943d34-2384-5dae-913e-74d32b4a024f" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323683 1074 "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323220 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323313 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1069 "q-354843" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1071 "A runner moves along a straight track relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-2t-12,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. The positive direction is left to right. If the runner is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time interval in which the runner moves to the left.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$<t<$\n\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1072 #uuid "85cebeda-e292-5b54-a79e-e10c4c5b6233" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323684 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t-12)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(4)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}-12(4)+C \\\\ 0 & =64-16-48+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the runner at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the runner is moving to the *left*, we must solve the inequality $v<0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-12t=t(t-4)(t+3)<0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t)$, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/27/27b9a41b0ad5c3ff98f6d76e24f0dc74e863d8f63a4c033fc41b7f7f2ebd72ea.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v<0$ whenever $0\\,<\\,t\\,<\\,4.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323408 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1069 "q-35489" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1$, find its $y$-intercepts." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1072 #uuid "40f09dba-8d73-5062-813a-36456c356837" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323685 1074 "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}-1 & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =1\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and $1+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,1-\\sqrt{5},0)$ and $(0,1+\\sqrt{5},0).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323468 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323575 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1069 "q-354997" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1072 #uuid "9b43bcef-d3e4-53de-9882-a31e4b89dee0" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323686 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =0 \\\\ 4t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-48\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-48\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-48<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$  Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =3 \\\\ 0+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =3+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323461 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323535 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1069 "q-354998" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1072 #uuid "fdcb4a35-fcad-5ff2-b2a2-88ba2f60d1cc" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323687 1074 "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$  \n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =0 \\\\ 5t & =\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.  \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{2\\pi }{5},\\frac{3\\pi }{5},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =50\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{5})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ Therefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C & =2 \\\\ -2+C & =2 \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{max}} & =v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity  ${v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323210 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1069 "q-35513" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1072 #uuid "8f50f445-3873-5d48-8eec-e37880dda36a" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323688 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323644 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1069 "q-355266" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{2}{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t={e}^{3}\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1072 #uuid "47b8ca7a-edf5-5fb5-8660-faa599afefc3" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323689 1074 "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v({e}^{3})=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+C \\\\ 4 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2.$ \n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=e\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323335 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1069 "q-355525" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1072 #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323690 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323637 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1069 "q-355528" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\sqrt{y}$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(1)=9.$  Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1072 #uuid "e4bca2e9-3423-5b05-8658-606cf78e9ed4" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323691 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & =2{x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ 