the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x-3}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2x+3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323443 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1069 "q-3617" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1072 #uuid "acee9da4-09d4-567b-82f2-79a4b4042b4a" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323765 1074 "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a discriminant that is not a perfect square:\n\n$$\nD={4}^{2}-4(1)(-1)=20\n$$\n\nWe have that $\\sqrt{20}$ is not a rational number, which implies that the quadratic factor doesn't factor over the rational numbers.\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+9x-4=A({x}^{2}+4x-1)+(Bx+C)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=2,B=1$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+4x-1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+9x-4}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+4x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{x+2