gral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(3+4{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[3+\\frac{4}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(5+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[5\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+1]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi +4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323522 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1069 "q-361710" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1072 #uuid "e7e02b8c-3f1c-501d-8c41-5eeda979ab48" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323767 1074 "Recall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{sin}^{2}⁡x+11{cos}^{2}⁡x & =5{sin}^{2}⁡x+5{cos}^{2}⁡x+6{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =5({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+6{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =5+6{cos}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(5+6{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[5+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(8+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(8x+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})]-[8(0)+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))] \\\\  & =[4\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )]-[0+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[4\\pi +\\frac{3}{2}(0)]-[0+\\frac{3}{2}(0)] \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323216 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1069 "q-361711" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1071 "![](images/f8/f8aa97ff1ff27ec11df6aefd4523ed9d39efac686c777f7242701aa2f4fef79c.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, as shown above. The area of the region is\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1072 #uuid "292b6739-634d-583f-b073-f74b62cabddf" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323768 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate this integral, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)+2-x]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+3-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}]-[0] \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323650 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1069 "q-361717" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1071 "![](images/fc/fc09b3b302a0920562a3ca03f52a2f6f98dc327b89f231e07b8a6ab631a48b2b.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1072 #uuid "38bf9886-fe53-5f33-902e-5daa5808ccd5" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323769 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [1,3]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}+{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[{3}^{4}-{1}^{4}]+[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[81-1]+[27-1] \\\\  & =-20+26 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}-{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{4}^{4}-{3}^{4}]-[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[256-81]-[64-27] \\\\  & =\\frac{175}{4}-37 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{4}+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323528 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1069 "q-361718" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1071 "Find the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-16$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1072 #uuid "d6c8a9d9-98ac-5f9b-a80f-198e1bbaf034" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323770 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [0,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](images/bf/bf221ae10e68a9c17efadd82b39264807b4dc00774fae7e30d9c0c9907368698.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative.\n\nTherefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+16{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+16x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[{2}^{5}-{0}^{5}]+16[2-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(32-0)+16(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+32 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{160}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [2,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-16{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}-16x\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[{3}^{5}-{2}^{5}]-16[3-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(243-32)-16(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{5}-16 \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{5}-\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{131}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}+\\frac{131}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323666 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1069 "q-36178" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+8z=0 \\\\ 2x-5y+4z=3 \\\\ 5x-4y+7z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & a \\\\ 2 & b & 4 \\\\ 5 & c & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1072 #uuid "075fd7f5-bea7-5040-92fa-5b19399df625" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323771 1074 "We can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=8,\\,b=-5,\\,c=-4.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323352 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1069 "q-3619" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1072 #uuid "2f0631c5-dc78-5feb-807b-cc910eff2885" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323772 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{x-3}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)(x-3)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=A(x-3)^{2}+B(x-3)(x-2)+C(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{2} & =A(3-3)^{2}+B(3-3)(3-2)+C(3-2) \\\\ 9 & =C \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$  we can eliminate  $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2} & =A(2-3)^{2}+B(2-3)(2-2)+C(2-2) \\\\ 4 & =A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2} & =A(0-3)^{2}+B(0-3)(0-2)+C(0-2) \\\\ 0 & =9A+6B-2C \\\\ 0 & =9(4)+6B-2(9) \\\\ 0 & =18+6B \\\\ -6B & =18 \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(x-2)(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{3}{x-3}+\\frac{9}{(x-3)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+9\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\frac{9}{x-3}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323668 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1069 "q-36192" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1071 "Let $A$ be the set of all multiples of $2$ greater than $0$ and let $B$ be the set of all multiples of $3$ greater than $0.$ What is $A∖B?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1072 #uuid "d4bb504c-7282-5ccd-a89f-3ae399678bda" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323773 1074 "The multiples of $2$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16,18,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $3$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB={3,6,9,12,15,18,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A∖B$ is the *difference* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $A$ but *not* in $B.$\n\nThe elements of $A$ that are also in $B$ are $6,12,18,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $6$). So, we must remove these elements from $A.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∖B & =A-B \\\\  & ={2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16,18,…}-{3,6,9,12,15,18,…} \\\\  & ={2,4,8,10,14,16,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323410 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1069 "q-362157" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1072 #uuid "a618cdf1-860e-5928-99cb-d500520f67b4" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323774 1074 "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{u}{|}_{1}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =2-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323616 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1069 "q-36217" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1072 #uuid "7b4e3e26-9c93-555b-9869-caeb48ae4f9d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323775 1074 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+4\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323351 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1069 "q-36227" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1072 #uuid "0c3c463e-b1e6-5da9-a9e8-9ab02a63ca2d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323776 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323377 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1069 "q-36244" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1071 "To which of the following sets do all the real numbers belong?\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$\n- $N$\n- $Q$\n- ${Q}^{-}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1072 #uuid "f51862bd-578b-533f-86e5-e297bb989e9a" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323777 1074 "- The set ${Z}^{-}$ contains all the negative integer numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set $N$ contains all the natural numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to $N,$ so $N$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set $Q$ contains all the rational numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to $Q,$ so $Q$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set ${Q}^{-}$ contains all the negative rational numbers. However, real numbers as, for instance, $\\sqrt{2}$ don't belong to ${Q}^{-},$ so ${Q}^{-}$ does *not* contain the real numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323373 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1069 "q-36248" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1071 "To which of the following sets do all the positive integers belong?\n\n- $N$\n- ${R}^{+}$\n- ${Q}^{-}$\n- ${Z}^{-}$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1072 #uuid "4fc9ff9e-591c-5172-b83a-e2856d63bd94" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323778 1074 "The positive integer numbers are $1,2,3,…$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are natural numbers, all the positive integers belong to $N.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Since all positive integer numbers are positive real numbers, all the positive integer numbers belong to ${R}^{+}.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The set ${Q}^{-}$ contains all the negative rational numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Q}^{-},$ so ${Q}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\n- The set ${Z}^{-}$ contains all the negative integer numbers. However, positive integer numbers as, for instance, $2$ don't belong to ${Z}^{-},$ so ${Z}^{-}$ does *not* contain the positive integer numbers. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323221 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1069 "q-362509" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1072 #uuid "39b5bd8a-dd36-5527-89de-e36f567f0906" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323779 1074 "Using the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{1-{tan}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}{(1-{tan}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323589 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1069 "q-362529" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{cot}^{2}⁡x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1072 #uuid "f6e12ad2-92d7-59aa-b273-12c735cff58d" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323780 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{cot}^{2}⁡x+4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)+4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+3(1)+4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x+7}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+7}-\\sqrt{1+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}-\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323582 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1069 "q-362530" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1072 #uuid "0a3e4c61-3446-503d-9221-974875074079" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323781 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity\n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{4}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323333 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1069 "q-362543" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [3,6].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1072 #uuid "5dea3fa8-c6cf-5814-afce-4f5dbc98cc0c" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323782 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2},$ $a=3,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\sqrt{x-3}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x-2}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3/2})|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(6-2)^{3/2}]-[\\frac{2}{3}(3-2)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323260 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1069 "q-362546" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1072 #uuid "6e12233f-3a34-57d8-bf38-b36992ad1033" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323783 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(2-{x}^{2})^{-1/2}(-2x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{2-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}))|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})]-[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}})] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323308 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1069 "q-362547" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1071 "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x+2)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,6].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1072 #uuid "67a2b624-03df-55f0-a841-a36eb3ef7584" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323784 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x+2)^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x+2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\sqrt{x+2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x+3}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}\\sqrt{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}(x+3)^{3/2})|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(6+3)^{3/2}]-[\\frac{2}{3}(1+3)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323525 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1069 "q-362589" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1072 #uuid "22f34c0a-d2be-545d-8a94-5b6c02bfb19b" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323785 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ $a=-1,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1-2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1-x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1+x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+1}-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{2})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323658 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1069 "q-362590" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1071 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1072 #uuid "2ab5bb68-6c78-5933-86bc-f3182668702a" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323786 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1+2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1+x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{