^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{-x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})\\,(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation for $I,$ we solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}I \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}I & =-{e}^{-x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}(0-2))-(-\\frac{1}{5}(0+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi /2}}{5}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(1+{e}^{-\\pi /2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323223 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323788 1069 "q-362669" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323788 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}