c{-4}{1}=-4.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323652 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1069 "q-37406" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1072 #uuid "774c9d47-2d15-5219-81a5-dce5b7c23a5f" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323921 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323266 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1069 "q-3742" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1071 "$4-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1072 #uuid "f2673630-1831-5e8a-88b1-e8701b811418" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323922 1074 "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $q$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{q}{q}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q-(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q+2}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2q+1)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323422 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1069 "q-37449" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1071 "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(4,8)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1072 #uuid "14dd51d6-4da8-56bc-8036-6e67390713d4" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323923 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323243 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1069 "q-37516" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1071 "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1072 #uuid "12b276e2-b703-55f7-94b8-069326b3f510" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323924 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323460 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1069 "q-37527" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$\n- $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1072 #uuid "26a6234c-3080-57fe-a159-1f4332413072" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323925 1074 "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323649 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1069 "q-37577" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1071 "$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $G$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3?$" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1072 #uuid "5ed416fd-31fc-5c4a-aaad-80e5dc0cc476" 13194139533421 17592186045585 true] [17592186323926 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(G-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 